A||||8||Formula 18||Black88GT||||1||
Z||000000||Formula 18||09-29-2000||04:59 AM||||Thank you in advance. <P>You mentioned that 93 rotors off a cavlier would fit the back of a 88, but i have two questions. <P>One-&gt; I thought that the front and rear rotors were interchangable, if so why would these rotors only work on the back? <P>Two-&gt; have you tried this, if so how'd did it work? Results, and a little how-to would be awesome. <P><BR>Thanks again <BR>Brian ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Black88GT||09-29-2000||05:13 AM||||No problem.  I asked the same question to Randy Agee, the guy who told me this....<P>"The 93 Cavalier rotor WILL NOT fit the front of the 88 because of the larger size wheel bearing hub used on the front"<P>Also, he mentioned this which I thought was informative and beneficial...<P>"If you swap out the quick take up valve from your old 88 Master Cylinder with<BR>the one in a MC from a 93 S-10/S-15  you will have a replacement for the 88 MC for a<BR>lot less money.  You can also use the S-10 MC "as is" but since the take up valve<BR>is different the rear calipers do not stay "loaded".  Other than that, it will<BR>function fine.<P>An 85-87 Fiero Master cylinder can also be used "in a pinch".  The bore of these MCs is slightly larger than the 88, so effort is "slightly" increased - but the<BR>brakes will work fine.  Some may actually prefer this set-up as pedal travel is<BR>also slightly reduced.<P>The S-10 MC is the same bore diameter as the 88,  is alloy, and has the same mechanical connections.  It is also a whole lot less expensive"<P>And when I asked about the quality of the Autozone rotors...<P>"The Autozone rotors are made in China.  We have found no problems, other than an<BR>occasional variance in rotor thickness where it slips over the wheel stubs, with<BR>them"<P><BR>hope that helps... oh yeah I have not tried this but I plan on it soon...<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||14
Z||000002||Black88GT||09-29-2000||09:19 AM||||Oh yeah, I almost forgot,the Cavalier rotors are NOT identical to the Fiero rotors....<BR>||||reg||3
Z||000003||Formula 18||09-29-2000||10:58 AM||||What is the Differance? Are they still vented rotors? ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Cooter||09-30-2000||07:38 PM||||I picked up a pair of 93 Cavalier rotors today. Outside diameter is the same as the Fiero rotors. The problem with using them on the front is the Cavalier uses a smaller hub where the lug studs come through. The lug pattern is the same, just the Fiero hubs have a larger diameter. <P>However! Some creative use of a bench grinder on the outside diameter of the Fiero hubs and a little die grinder action on the back of the rotor to make it clear the dust shield and I have a new pair of rotors on the FRONT of my 88 for only $32. Yes- it did take a while, but next time the job should be an almost bolt on. It can be done, just be patient.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Black88GT||10-01-2000||05:49 PM||||Thats cool.  I think I am going to buy them now.  $32 rotors at Autozone for 88 fiero's, who says '88 parts are hard to find and expensive?!?!?<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||7
Z||000006||skitime||10-01-2000||10:09 PM||||I just installed the Cavalier rotors on our 88 Gt rear.  They work great. The Cavalier rotor center hole is a few thousands larger than the Fiero's center hole but is not a problem.  I found an easy way to deal with the emergency brake in the caliber.  If you remove the brake arm from the caliber, you can press the piston in just like normal calibers.  No more trying to get the spanner wrench to fit the piston or figuring how to turn the piston. I will be turning the rear rotors to put on the front since they have little wear compared to the front rotor. ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Tom88gt||11-15-2000||09:06 PM||||Cooter, how much material did you have to remove from the front hubs and cavalier rotors?  You idea is interesting but what are the risks?  As you remove material, you weaken the part.  Also, how did it turn out?  Any vibration or pulsating pedal?  Do they stop well?||||reg||5
Z||000008||Cooter||11-15-2000||10:21 PM||||If I weakened the hub by removing the outer diameter- it is now just as strong as the Cavalier hub  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>It took a while to do because I took my time to make sure that nothing got over heated in the process. So far, so good! The front hubs were in poor shape to begin with, so when I replace them, I will probably just get some new FieroStore rotors so I won't have to grind the new hubs. As far as I can tell, the car has GREAT brakes for a 12 year old.||||reg||1
